<p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Motivation"></a> Motivation </h2>
<p>
There are three main reasons for adapting the matlab code to Fortran.=20
The first reason is the suspicion that a Fortran version of the code=20
would run faster than a matlab version, and indeed benchmarks suggest=20
the Fortran implementation is some 25-30% faster, depending on the=20
compiler. The second reason is that the parallelization problem is=20
easier to deal with in Fortran, in this author's opinion, since the=20
suite of communication subroutines from MPI can be easily employed. The=20
parallel version of this code is quite easy to manage. And the third=20
reason is to give some flexibility to adapting the code to other=20
implementations, e.g., the possible use of <a href=3D"http://www.nvidia.com=
/object/cuda_home.html" target=3D"_blank">Cuda<img alt=3D"" src=3D"https://=
staff.washington.edu/dushaw/AcousticsCode/RamFortranCode_files/external-lin=
k.gif" border=3D"0" height=3D"12" width=3D"13"></a>
 for massive parallelization of RAM calculations, using the 100 or more=20
processors of modern graphics cards as a co-processor, is intriguing.
</p><p>
Fortran 95 was used because this user is most familiar with Fortran;=20
matlab code is astonishingly similar to Fortran 95. In addition, Fortran
 95 allows for arrays to be allocated on the fly to any size, which is a
 very convenient feature.
</p><p>
This code is designed to run with the suite of subroutines from the=20
"Message Passing Interface" system (MPI) on a cluster, hence its name,=20
MPIRAM. There is a single processor version for simple situations,=20
however.
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Single_Double_Precision"></a> Single/Double Precision </=
h2>
<p>
In June/July 2013, I learned that there is no inherent reason why RAM,=20
or PE calculations in general, should be all in 100% double precision=20
(Kevin Heaney, Kevin Smith, Richard Campbell, Mike Porter, to name a few
 who confirmed this...). A few reconfigurations later produced mpiramS,=20
the single precision version, which works very well for computations out
 to a few megameter range. It developed that for small deltaz, required=20
for an accurate prediction of time fronts, some pesky numerical noise=20
arose. This noise was traced to variables in the subroutine matrc, so=20
that the new code is set up to be mostly single precision, with double=20
precision used for key elements. The documentation for RAM by Collins=20
notes this. Using mostly single precision gives calculations adequate=20
for most cases, at about half the computational cost.  In addition,=20
intel's hyperthreading technology becomes very effective with this=20
single precision code - going from 4 CPUs to 8 hyperthreaded processors=20
gives a ca. 40% boost in computation.
</p><p>
One can convert this code to 100% double precision by editing the=20
src/kinds.f90 module and changing the precision for "wp" to=20
"kind(1.0d0)". Change the reads of the matlab script plotram.m to real*8
 from real*4 as well. Or change to 100% single precision by changing the
 precision for "wp2" to "kind(1.0e0).
</p><p>
With this reconfiguration, the requirement for lapack/blas elements was=20
discarded in favor of the original gaussian elimination subroutine=20
employed by Collins.
</p><p>
The code was also redesigned slightly to allow for parallelization using
 openmp. One can now use either openmp or MPI. The MPI version seems to=20
run slightly, 4%, faster on a single CPU.   MPI also allows many=20
computers to be brought to bear on the task. Note that changing the=20
precision to all double precision requires changes to the MPI calling=20
routines in peramx_mpi.f90 to MPI_Double_Precision rather than MPI_FLOAT
 or things will crash.
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Philosophy"></a> Philosophy </h2>
<p>
This code, and the other codes for acoustic propagation listed at the=20
top of this page, is designed for deep-water, long-range, low-frequency,
 broad-band acoustic propagation. The codes may well work for=20
shallow-water, short-range, and high frequencies, but the author of this
 code makes no representation that it is good for those situations.
</p><p>
You should be approaching this code with the notion that you will be=20
adapting it to suit your own specific purpose. There are too many=20
complicated issues involved with calculating acoustic propagation with=20
the RAM code to be able to put together a one-size-fits-all suite of=20
code. Issues range from whether internal waves are being modeled or not,
 which affects the spatial resolutions implemented in the code, to=20
whether and how bottom interactions need to be modeled, which is beyond=20
the scope of this small page. So, this code should be viewed as a set of
 parts, put together in some generic, example-type way, that you will=20
need to tune to your own needs.
</p><p>
Because the Fortran 95 coding is similar to matlab, it should be easily=20
adaptable to a specific purpose. The output from this code is also=20
designed to easily integrate into a standard matlab working environment;
 a matlab script to read the direct binary file written by the code is=20
provided.
</p><p>
The design of the code makes use of Fortran 95 modules to define common=20
areas of data that are shared between the main program and its=20
subroutines. These modules take the place of COMMON blocks of FORTRAN=20
77, but are far easier to deal with. These modules are: <em>envdata.f90, ma=
ttri.f90, param.f90</em>, and <em>profiles.f90</em>.
 The effect is that one does not have to pass variables to subroutines=20
in long lists; rather, the data are available already to the subroutine.
 <em>kinds.f90</em> and <em>interpolators.f90</em> are also modules, but th=
ey are used for other purposes.
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Compilation_Notes"></a> Compilation Notes </h2>
<p>
Compiling the code should be relatively straightforward - the code has=20
compiled successfully on g95, gfortran, Intel's ifort compiler, and the=20
Sun Studio compiler. (Of these the Sun compiler produced the fastest=20
executable for the longest time, but gfortran 5.0 now seems to be the=20
best one to use.) Makefile's are provided for all components of the=20
code. The first task is for the user to take a look at the Makefile's=20
and edit them for their intended compilers.
</p><h3><a name=3D"Compiling_LAPACK"></a> Compiling LAPACK </h3>
<p>
The code need to solve a small matrix problem in epade.f90. In older=20
versions of the code, LAPACK routines were been set up to do that.=20
Either a standard LAPACK library can be linked to the code, or, for=20
portability, the small subset of LAPACK routines needed to do the=20
calculation are included in the tarball in the subdirectory "lapack".=20
Executing "make" in that directory should produce an archive <span style=3D=
"color: #ff0000">smlapack.a</span> (for "small lapack") that can be linked =
to the code. This is a very small calculation; efficiency is a non-issue he=
re.
</p><p>
This requirement is not needed with the more recent mpiramS.
</p><h3><a name=3D"Compiling_MPIRAM_single_processo"></a> Compiling MPIRAM =
- single processor </h3>
<p>
Just executing "make" should make the single processor binary, with the res=
ulting executable <span style=3D"color: #ff0000">s_mpiram</span>, for "sing=
le mpiram". "make clean" will delete the object (./obj/*.o) and module (./m=
od/*.mod) files.
</p><p>
With the more recent mpiramS, this compilation can employ openmp to bring m=
ultiprocessor CPUs to bear on the task.
</p><h3><a name=3D"Compiling_MPIRAM_MPI_processing"></a> Compiling MPIRAM -=
 MPI processing </h3>
<p>
The first step to compiling MPIRAM to do calculations in parallel is to=20
install MPI, configuring it to use a Fortran 95 compiler. A description=20
of how to do that is beyond the scope of this document. "See Also" below
 gives a link to the MPICH implementation of MPI.
</p><p>
The Makefile for the MPI version is "Makefile.mpi" - take a look at this
 file to adjust the various compiler flags and lapack library for your=20
own situation. The compiler to use is "mpif90", which is a product of=20
your MPI installation and the compiler you configured it to use; the=20
compiler flags in Makefile.mpi should be tuned to your Fortran compiler.
 Then "make -f Makefile.mpi" should compile the modules and code and=20
produce the <span style=3D"color: #ff0000">mpiram</span> binary
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"The_Input_File_in_pe"></a> The Input File: <span style=
=3D"color: #0000ff">in.pe</span> </h2>
<p>
A prerequisite to putting together a reasonable input file is the documenta=
tion on the various parameters given on the <a href=3D"https://staff.washin=
gton.edu/dushaw/AcousticsCode/RamMatlabCode.html">RAM in Matlab</a>
 page. Critical parameters are T (time window), deltar (range=20
increment), and deltaz (depth increment). The discussion of the nature=20
of the parameters will not be repeated here.
</p><p>
When the binary runs, it takes no input from the console, but looks for a
 file "in.pe" to read in the parameter and filename information it=20
needs. Here is an example:
</p><p>
<strong>Example in.pe File</strong>
</p><pre>                    !  input file to PE - CAUTION:  formatted inpu=
t!
75   2              !  center frequency (Hz), fc, and Q value; bandwidth=3D=
fc/Q
2.0                 !  time window width (s) - expected arrival pattern dur=
ation
1000.0              !  source depth (m)
30000.000           !  receiver range (m)
0.5                 !  deltaz - an accuracy parameter (m)  (0.5 is liberal)
250.0               !  deltar - an accuracy parameter (m)  (250 is liberal)
4 1                 !  np-# pade coefficients, ns-#stability terms
10000.0             !  rs - stability range (m); ns=3D0 for shorter ranges
50                  !  output depth  decimation (integer) (decrease if you =
get ripples in your matlab plots)
test.ssp            !  Input the soundspeed filename (for single processor,=
 use "munk" for munk canonical)
1                   !  Flat earth transformation: 0=3Dno, 1=3Dyes
1                   !  Horizontal linear interpolation flag: 0=3Dno, 1=3Dye=
s
0                   !  Enable ocean bottom? 0=3Dno, 1=3Dyes (yes turns on r=
eading the file)
test.bth            !  Input the bathymetry filename (not used if ocean bot=
tom =3D 0 above)
1                   !  Flag for node weighting when computing on a cluster.=
  Set to 0 to evenly weight.
nodewts.dat         !  File name for node weights (not used if flag is 0).<=
/pre>
<p>
The user should adjust these parameters for his own circumstance, taking ca=
re to respect the format of the file. ( <strong>To
 enable the comments on the right, formatted input is used - keep the=20
same number of decimal places in the numbers and don't let the=20
exclamation marks get too far to the left, where they might be picked up
 by the code.</strong>)
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Running_MPIRAM"></a> Running MPIRAM </h2>
<p>
To run the single processor version of mpiram just execute the binary <span=
 style=3D"color: #ff0000">./s_mpiram</span>. It will read the <span style=
=3D"color: #0000ff">in.pe</span> input file, do the calculation, and write =
out two files: <span style=3D"color: #0000ff">psif.dat</span> a direct acce=
ss binary file with the results and <span style=3D"color: #0000ff">recl.dat=
</span>
 which stores the value of the record length of the direct access file=20
in ascii. The latter is needed to be able to properly read the binary=20
file later.
</p><p>
</p><h3><a name=3D"Running_the_MPI_binary"></a> Running the MPI binary </h3=
>
<p>
First, be sure to copy the <span style=3D"color: #ff0000">mpiram</span>=20
executable binary to all the remote locations, to a location that is in=20
your search path; I like to copy it to /home/yourusername/bin/. On the=20
aogreef.ucsd.edu cluster, the filesystem is mounted NFS so the binary is
 available to all nodes by default.
</p><p>
To run the MPI version of the binary, the code needs a bit of a wrapper aro=
und MPI: <em>time mpiexec -n 11 time mpiram</em> will run the <span style=
=3D"color: #ff0000">mpiram</span> executable on 11 nodes and time the execu=
tion on each node and for the job as a whole. As for the single processor v=
ersion, <span style=3D"color: #ff0000">mpiram</span> will read the <span st=
yle=3D"color: #0000ff">in.pe</span> input file. The convenient script <em>r=
unit</em> is provided that contains this line. &lt;Control&gt;-&lt;c&gt; wi=
ll abort the job on all nodes, if necessary.
</p><p>
As for the single processor version, the code will write out two files on c=
ompletion of the calculation: <span style=3D"color: #0000ff">psif.dat</span=
> a direct access binary file with the results and <span style=3D"color: #0=
000ff">recl.dat</span> which stores the value of the record length of the d=
irect access file in ascii.
</p><p>
For small values of <em>deltaz</em> on an SMP machine, the calculations=20
do not seem to scale well with the number of CPUs, and one might be=20
better off running fewer jobs than there are CPU's. On my quad-core=20
systems, 3 jobs per system is more efficient than 4; it can take a bit=20
of experimenting to settle on the optimal configuration.
</p><h3><a name=3D"Load_balancing"></a> Load balancing </h3>
<p>
The last entries in the "in.pe" file allow the calculation to assign=20
blocks of frequencies based on the computational ability of each=20
processor of the cluster. This is useful if one has an eclectic cluster=20
consisting of older and newer processors. Set the flag to be 1 for the=20
code to read in the weighting values from a file, or 0 to just assign=20
equal number of frequencies to all.
</p><p>
To assign the weights to each node, just use the time required to=20
calculate a single frequency as reported by mpiram (See "Benchmarks"=20
below). The code will then do a simple calculation to assign more=20
frequencies to faster processors. One can optimize the calculation by=20
fiddling with the numbers to assign more or fewer frequencies to each=20
node.
</p><p>
Here is an example of an input file:
</p><pre>56     % Four cpus of node 1
56     % Set the value to be the approximate compute time for one frequency=
.
55
55
87     % Four slower cpus of node 2=20
87     % mpdtrace gives the order of the nodes
87
87
54     % Four cpus of node 3
54
55
55
45     % Two faster cpus of node 4 (a quadcore Q6600 that does not scale we=
ll to four processors)
45</pre>
<p>
The order of the nodes is as reported by "mpitrace -l". mpiram will=20
write out the node names and number of frequencies assigned to each. The
 user should check that the number of frequencies are assigned to the=20
nodes in the right order.
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Working_With_the_Results_in_Matl"></a> Working With the =
Results in Matlab </h2>
<p>
We live in a matlab world these days, and the MPIRAM source code=20
distribution includes a small matlab script "plotram.m" that will read=20
and plot up the results of the calculation. Most of that script has been
 borrowed from Matt's matlab version.
</p><p>
The results of the calculation are written as direct access binary file,
 and they include a collection of essential parameters (frequency, time=20
window width, Q, etc.) and the complex full acoustic field. To read the=20
direct access file, the matlab script first reads the file written by=20
MPIRAM named <em>recl.dat</em>, which has the size of the record length=20
of the binary file. It develops that this number is not the same among=20
different compilers, e.g., for the intel compiler it has to be=20
multiplied by a factor of 4 for the matlab reads to work properly. So=20
getting the correct correspondence between the MPIRAM and matlab record=20
lengths may take some minor fiddling.
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Input_Sound_Speed_Vertical_and_H"></a> Input Sound Speed=
: Vertical and Horizontal Interpolation </h2>
<p>
It can't be stressed enough that the user should be responsible for=20
giving this code sound speed realizations that have adequate, accurate=20
resolution to begin with. The code does some interpolation, i.e., has a=20
certain model for how to treat horizontal and vertical variations, but=20
that treatment should not be relied upon for accuracy.
</p><p>
For calculations involving fine-scale variability, e.g., internal waves=20
or sharp ocean fronts, the sound speed sections input to the code should
 have the appropriate horizontal and vertical resolution to begin with.
</p><p>
</p><h3><a name=3D"Horizontal_Interpolation"></a> Horizontal Interpolation =
</h3>
<p>
The RAM model has no inherent way to treat range dependence. When the=20
code is given a range-dependent sound speed section, it just marches=20
along in range, taking the nearest sound speed profile. If coarse=20
resolution sound speed are given to the code, rather dramatic results=20
make arise from possible abrupt changes to the sound speed profile as=20
the code jumps from one profile to the next. In effect, such abrupt=20
changes may lead to aggressive and unphysical mode coupling.
</p><p>
Short of sending the code adequate sound speed sections, the Fortran 95=20
version of the code will optionally perform a linear interpolation of=20
the sound speed section horizontally to make the changes to the sound=20
speed profiles more gradual. The model in mind here is if one wanted a=20
quick PE calculation using a section derived from the World Ocean Atlas.
</p><p>
If you wanted none of this horizontal interpolation, you may change the=20
flag in the input file to turn it off. If you wanted the code to=20
calculate horizontal resolution differently, then change the relevant=20
code in the peramx.f90 source code.
</p><p>
</p><h3><a name=3D"Vertical_Interpolation"></a> Vertical Interpolation </h3=
>
<p>
The RAM propagation technique needs to interpolate the sound speed onto a d=
epth grid with spacing <em>deltaz</em>.
 The original version of RAM, including the matlab version, did this=20
interpolation by linear interpolation in the "gorp" function. This=20
simple interpolation made it essential to input fine-scale sound speed=20
profiles to the code. For ranges O(1000 km) a value of deltaz of 0.25 m=20
or less is appropriate, hence the vertical interpolation is to very fine
 scale, indeed.
</p><p>
For little additional computational cost, the Fortran 95 version of the cod=
e implements a cubic spline interpolation onto the <em>deltaz</em>
 grid. For the same input sound speed section, this author believes the=20
cubic spline approach will be slightly more accurate. At the same time,=20
for simple calculations such as sound speed sections derived from the=20
World Ocean Atlas, this Fortran 95 version of RAM will give a fairly=20
accurate result even if coarse-resolution sound speed profiles are=20
input. Similarly, the vertical resolution of the input sound speeds can=20
be irregular, perhaps with high resolution near the surface, and lower=20
resolution deeper. Considerable memory is also saved, since sound speeds
 with superfine resolution throughout the water column are not required.
</p><p>
However, it is stressed again that this interpolation inherent in the=20
model should not be relied upon to give accurate vertical interpolation -
 rather, the interpolation should be viewed as just part of the=20
machinery to do the calculation. Be sure to give the code adequately=20
resolved sound speed profiles, and bear in mind that the code will be=20
doing a cubic spline to achieve the <em>deltaz</em> -spaced grid.
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Notes_on_the_Sea_Floor"></a> Notes on the Sea Floor </h2=
>
<p>
The code implements a very simple model for the ocean bottom and the=20
acoustic interaction with it. No shear is modeled, and acoustic energy=20
that interacts with the bottom is rather excessively attenuated (0.5=20
dB/wavelength). The code is generally designed for ocean-only=20
propagation. Nevertheless, there are situations when it would be helpful
 to have some notion of what bottom reflected energy might look like,=20
and this code might be able to do that. For more accurate calculations=20
involving the ocean bottom, there are other versions of RAM that might=20
be more appropriate (RAMGEO or RAMS).
</p><p>
</p><table rules=3D"all" class=3D"twikiTable" id=3D"table1" border=3D"1" ce=
llpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0">
		<tbody><tr>
			<td bgcolor=3D"#ffffff" valign=3D"top"> <img alt=3D"sediment_properties.=
png" src=3D"https://staff.washington.edu/dushaw/AcousticsCode/RamFortranCod=
e_files/sediment_properties.png" height=3D"348" width=3D"450"> </td>
		</tr>
		<tr class=3D"twikiTableEven twikiTableRowdataBgSorted1 twikiTableRowdataB=
g1">
			<td bgcolor=3D"#edf4f9" valign=3D"top"> Some nominal properties of the s=
ea floor. "p" refers to compressional speeds, <br> "s" refers to shear spee=
ds. As listed here, basalt has an attenuation of 0.1 dB/&#955;, <br> and at=
 100 Hz sound has a wavelength of 52.5 m. Therefore, attenuation of <br> 10=
0-Hz compressional waves in basalt is about 2 dB/km (19 wavelengths in a km=
). <br> From <em>Computational Acoustics</em> by Jensen, Kuperman, Porter, =
and Schmidt (1994), <br> originally from Hamilton. </td>
		</tr></tbody></table>
The default model for the sea floor in this package is a sound speed=20
that increases linearly from the sea floor by 200 m/s to a depth of=20
300-m below the sea floor. One can induce significant bottom-reflected=20
energy in two ways (a) decrease the bottom attenuation, in which case=20
the acoustic energy will penetrate into the sea floor to some extent,=20
refract, and then reenter the water column, or (b) set the sound speed=20
gradient below the sea floor to be very large, in which case the=20
acoustic energy will essentially reflect specularly from the sea floor.
<p>
</p><table id=3D"table2" class=3D"twikiTable" rules=3D"all" border=3D"1" ce=
llpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0">
		<tbody><tr>
			<td bgcolor=3D"#ffffff" valign=3D"top"> <img alt=3D"bottom_ssp.png" src=
=3D"https://staff.washington.edu/dushaw/AcousticsCode/RamFortranCode_files/=
bottom_ssp.png" height=3D"286" width=3D"350"> </td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td bgcolor=3D"#edf4f9" valign=3D"top">
 An example of a nominal sound speed profile below the seafloor. The=20
ocean-seafloor boundary lies at 4.5 km depth. This example has been=20
suggested as appropriate for the Philippine Sea, although it is crude in
 the extreme. </td>
		</tr></tbody></table>
The code accounts for variable topography in that the above model is=20
coded to follow any general bathymetric section input to the model.
<p>
Again, this model, together with parameters such as attenuation and=20
density, needs to be adjusted from their nominal values to something=20
suited to the user's purpose. Alternatively, the model for the sea floor
 may be entirely rewritten by the user, perhaps to include=20
range-dependent sea floor properties read in from a data file. The code=20
author is no expert in bottom interaction!
</p><p>
Since following the bathymetry requires that the code continuously=20
update the various matrices inherent to the calculation, turning on=20
variable bathymetry makes the calculation take considerably longer=20
(several times longer) than the flat bottom case. The subroutine=20
matrc.f90 is called whenever there is a change to the environment.
</p><p>
<img alt=3D"SharpRidge_sm.png" src=3D"https://staff.washington.edu/dushaw/A=
cousticsCode/RamFortranCode_files/SharpRidge_sm.png" height=3D"451" width=
=3D"600">
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Processor_Benchmarks"></a> Processor Benchmarks </h2>
<p>
<strong>Compute time for mpiram at 3 Mm range</strong> <br> The time for
 the test case included with the source code: range=3D3000 km, time=20
window=3D10 s, delta z=3D0.250, delta r=3D150; modestly range-dependent,=20
mid-latitude sound speed field. This particular benchmark is cache=20
dependent.
</p><p>
The benchmark cited is the time to complete one frequency; each=20
processor works on only one frequency at a time. To compute the time to=20
complete the entire task take the number of frequencies (749 in this=20
case), divide by the number of processors employed, and multiply by the=20
benchmark time. For example, on the Q6600 using four processors and the=20
older version of mpiram would take: (749/4)*125 s=3D23406 s, or 6.5 hrs,=20
but using only two processors it would take only: (749/2)*38.7=3D14493s,=20
or 4.0 hrs.  The new "hybrid" single/double precision version scales=20
much better on SMP machines, including making effective use of=20
hyperthreading and maximizing the performance of the AMD 8-core=20
processor.   Mpiram scales perfectly across the nodes of a cluster; it=20
has minimal inter-computer communication.
</p><p>
The compiler used here is gfortran 5.0 with general option "-Ofast=20
-march=3DXXXX"; gfortran 5.0 appears to compile noticeably faster binaries
 than older versions.   The MPI version employed because it is a several
 percent faster than the OMP version.  Faster RAM settings can greatly=20
improve performance, if you can get them stable. =20
</p><p>
